 975:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *
 976:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  The functions @c not1 and @c not2 each take a predicate functor
 977:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  and return an instance of @c unary_negate or
 978:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  @c binary_negate, respectively.  These classes are functors whose
 979:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  @c operator() performs the stored predicate function and then returns
 980:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  the negation of the result.
 981:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *
 982:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  For example, given a vector of integers and a trivial predicate,
 983:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  \code
 984:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  struct IntGreaterThanThree
