1058:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    * @ingroup functors
1059:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *
1060:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  The advantage of function objects over pointers to functions is that
1061:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  the objects in the standard library declare nested typedefs describing
1062:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  their argument and result types with uniform names (e.g., @c result_type
1063:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  from the base classes @c unary_function and @c binary_function).
1064:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  Sometimes those typedefs are required, not just optional.
1065:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *
1066:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  Adaptors are provided to turn pointers to unary (single-argument) and
1067:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  binary (double-argument) functions into function objects.  The
1068:c:\program files\daisytoolchain\arm-none-eabi\include\c++\10.2.1\bits\stl_function.h ****    *  long-winded functor @c pointer_to_unary_function is constructed with a
